    const Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content: 'A 50-year-old right-handed female former pediatric nurse presents to the PM&R clinic for mobility reassessment. She was diagnosed with relapsing-remitting MS at age 32 and transitioned to secondary progressive MS (SPMS) at age 44. Her current EDSS is 7.0 — she is essentially restricted to a wheelchair but can transfer and propel a manual wheelchair short distances. Over the past 6 months, she has become unable to walk even with a rolling walker, has fallen 6 times (sustaining a rib fracture from one fall), and is now using a borrowed manual wheelchair that is poorly fitted. She reports increasing upper extremity fatigue with manual wheelchair propulsion, worsening spasticity in both legs, neurogenic bladder requiring intermittent catheterization, and overwhelming fatigue that limits her to 3-4 productive hours per day. She was forced to leave her nursing career 3 years ago. She is divorced, lives alone in a two-story townhouse with the only bathroom upstairs, and her 22-year-old daughter lives across town. She enters the clinic in tears and says: "I used to take care of other people. Now I can\'t even take care of myself."',
