    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **MS disease course**: Age of diagnosis, number of relapses during RRMS phase, when progressive decline began (transition to SPMS), current disease-modifying therapy status. Has she been evaluated for any active inflammation (gadolinium-enhancing lesions) that might indicate SPMS with activity?\n- **Mobility decline timeline**: When did she transition from cane to walker to wheelchair? Rate of decline (gradual vs stepwise). Is there any remaining ambulation capacity (household ambulation, therapeutic standing)?\n- **Upper extremity function**: Grip strength, fine motor coordination, sensation. This is critical because upper extremity preservation determines independence with self-care, wheelchair propulsion, and catheterization. Any recent changes in hand function?\n- **Spasticity assessment**: Distribution (adductors, hamstrings, ankle plantarflexors), severity (Modified Ashworth Scale), functional impact (does spasticity interfere with transfers, catheterization, positioning?), current antispasticity medications, painful spasms (especially nocturnal).\n- **Bladder management**: Type of neurogenic bladder (urodynamics results), catheterization schedule, UTI frequency, fluid management, continence status. Has she seen urology recently?\n- **Bowel function**: Neurogenic bowel program, constipation management, bowel accidents, time required for bowel care.\n- **Fatigue**: Modified Fatigue Impact Scale (MFIS). Timing of fatigue (worse in afternoon, with heat). Current fatigue management strategies. Sleep quality — does nocturia disrupt sleep?\n- **Pain**: Neuropathic pain (burning, electric sensations), musculoskeletal pain from wheelchair use (shoulders, neck), spasticity-related pain.\n- **Cognitive function**: Processing speed, memory, executive function — cognitive impairment affects 40-70% of MS patients. Impact on medication management, appointment scheduling, self-care routines.\n- **Mood**: Depression screen (PHQ-9). Social isolation — she lives alone, lost her career, lost her marriage. This is a high-risk psychosocial profile.\n- **Skin integrity**: Pressure injury risk assessment — she is now primarily wheelchair-seated. Current cushion type and condition.\n- **Home environment**: Two-story townhouse with only bathroom upstairs — this is an immediate barrier to safety and independence.',
