    const Section(
      title: 'Physical Examination Findings:',
      content: '**Vitals**: BP 122/74 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, Temp 36.8C, Weight 156 lbs, BMI 27.2.\n\n**General**: Appears fatigued. Seated in a standard hospital-loaned manual wheelchair that is too wide, with a flat foam cushion. Posture shows mild thoracic kyphosis and left lateral lean. No pressure injuries visible.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. Affect is tearful but cooperative. Cognition grossly intact at bedside — formal neuropsychological testing recommended. Processing speed appears mildly reduced.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) on left lateral gaze (chronic, previously documented). Visual acuity 20/30 OS (history of optic neuritis). Remaining cranial nerves intact.\n- **Motor — Upper Extremities**: Deltoids 4+/5 bilaterally, biceps 5/5, triceps 4+/5, wrist extensors 4+/5, grip strength 22 kg right, 19 kg left. Fine motor: slightly impaired rapid finger movements. Intact ability to perform intermittent catheterization independently.\n- **Motor — Lower Extremities**: Hip flexors 2/5 bilaterally, knee extensors 2/5 right, 3-/5 left, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 2/5 bilaterally. Unable to perform straight leg raise against gravity on the right.\n- **Tone**: Modified Ashworth Scale — bilateral hip adductors 2, bilateral hamstrings 2, bilateral ankle plantarflexors 3. Bilateral ankle clonus (sustained, >10 beats). Spasticity interferes with passive ROM at the ankles and complicates transfer positioning.\n- **Sensory**: Reduced proprioception at bilateral ankles and toes. Reduced light touch and temperature sensation below the knees bilaterally. Intact sensation in upper extremities and trunk.\n- **Reflexes**: 3+ in bilateral lower extremities with bilateral Babinski. 2+ in upper extremities.\n- **Coordination**: Mild intention tremor bilateral hands. Impaired heel-to-shin (confounded by weakness).\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **Transfers**: Moderate assist x1 for stand-pivot transfer. Can perform lateral sliding board transfer with supervision and setup.\n- **Wheelchair Mobility**: Propels current manual wheelchair approximately 100 feet before upper extremity fatigue. Unable to propel up any incline. Cannot propel over outdoor surfaces (gravel, grass, broken sidewalk).\n- **ADLs**: Independent with upper body dressing. Requires assistance with lower body dressing due to spasticity and weakness. Independent with self-catheterization but reports increasing difficulty with positioning.\n- **EDSS**: 7.0 (restricted to wheelchair, able to transfer, can self-propel manual wheelchair limited distance).\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Mild erythema noted over bilateral ischial tuberosities — concerning for Stage 1 pressure injury or pre-injury. Current cushion is completely inadequate.',
