    const Section(
      title: '+ Differentiate SPMS from PPMS, and explain the clinical reasoning for determining when wheelchair transition is appropriate.',
      content: '**SPMS vs PPMS — Key Distinctions**:\n\n**Secondary Progressive MS (SPMS)**:\n- Begins as relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS) with clearly defined relapses and remissions\n- Transitions to progressive accumulation of disability independent of relapses\n- Transition is often gradual and recognized retrospectively — there is no definitive biomarker for the RRMS-to-SPMS transition\n- Can be classified as **SPMS with activity** (continued relapses or new MRI lesions superimposed on progressive decline) or **SPMS without activity**\n- This patient\'s course: RRMS diagnosed at 32, progressive decline without relapses beginning at 44 = SPMS\n- **Treatment**: Siponimod (Mayzent) is FDA-approved specifically for SPMS with active disease. Ocrelizumab may be considered. If no inflammatory activity, disease-modifying therapies have limited benefit and focus shifts to symptomatic management and rehabilitation.\n\n**Primary Progressive MS (PPMS)**:\n- Progressive accumulation of disability from onset — no initial relapsing phase\n- Approximately 10-15% of all MS patients\n- Tends to present later (average onset age 40s), more common equal male-to-female ratio (unlike RRMS which is 3:1 female)\n- Spinal cord involvement predominates (progressive myelopathy) with less brain lesion burden on MRI\n- **Treatment**: Ocrelizumab (Ocrevus) is the only FDA-approved disease-modifying therapy for PPMS\n\n**Clinical Determination of Wheelchair Transition**:\n\nThis is not a single decision point — it is a **spectrum of mobility aid progression** that should be assessed regularly:\n\n**Indicators that wheelchair evaluation is needed**:\n1. **Falls**: This patient has fallen 6 times in 6 months with a fracture — the risk-benefit ratio of continued ambulation has shifted toward danger. Recurrent falls are the clearest indicator.\n2. **Energy cost of ambulation**: When walking requires so much energy that the patient has no reserve for meaningful activities (work, social engagement, self-care), walking becomes an exercise rather than functional mobility.\n3. **Functional ambulation**: Can the patient ambulate safely within the home, in the community, and in time-critical situations (getting to the bathroom)? If not, ambulation is no longer functional.\n4. **EDSS 6.5-7.0**: This range typically marks the transition. EDSS 6.5 = constant bilateral assistance required. EDSS 7.0 = essentially wheelchair-dependent.\n5. **Upper extremity decline**: If UE function is declining, early power wheelchair provision preserves shoulder integrity for self-care and transfers rather than exhausting it on wheelchair propulsion.\n\n**Critical Reframing**: The wheelchair is not a sign of giving up — it is a **tool for expanded independence and safety**. Patients who resist wheelchair transition often experience accelerating decline from falls, fractures, deconditioning, and social isolation because they stop going places they cannot walk to.\n\n**For this patient**: She has clearly crossed the threshold. She cannot safely ambulate, she has sustained injuries from falls, and she is using a poorly fitted manual wheelchair that is causing fatigue and skin concerns. She needs a comprehensive wheelchair evaluation including power wheelchair assessment.',
    ),
    const Section(
