    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline the power wheelchair evaluation, home modification plan, upper body preservation program, and fatigue management strategy.',
      content: '**Power Wheelchair Evaluation and Prescription**:\n\n**Seating and Mobility Evaluation by ATP (Assistive Technology Professional)**:\n- Must be performed by a certified ATP in conjunction with a physiatrist or PT\n- **Power wheelchair with tilt-in-space and recline**: Essential for pressure redistribution, spasticity management (positioning), fatigue management, and postural support\n- **Seat width**: Custom-fit to patient — current wheelchair is too wide, causing lateral lean and poor posture\n- **Seat cushion**: Pressure-mapping assessment to select appropriate cushion. ROHO or Jay-series cushion with adequate pressure redistribution for ischial protection\n- **Back support**: Custom-molded or off-the-shelf with lateral trunk supports to correct lateral lean and thoracic kyphosis\n- **Footrests**: Angle-adjustable to accommodate ankle plantarflexor spasticity\n- **Headrest**: If fatigue causes head drop or for tilt/recline positioning\n- **Joystick control**: Standard hand joystick is appropriate given her current UE function. Contingency plan for alternative drive controls (head array, sip-and-puff) if UE function declines.\n- **Additional features**: Elevating legrests for edema management, seat elevator for counter-height access, USB charger port\n\n**Manual Wheelchair**: Retain a lightweight manual wheelchair for situations where power wheelchair cannot be used (small spaces, transport, backup). Ultralight rigid frame with ergonomic pushrim for short-distance propulsion.\n\n**Home Modification Plan — Immediate Priorities**:\n1. **Bathroom access crisis**: She lives in a two-story townhouse with the only bathroom upstairs — this is unsafe and unsustainable. Options:\n   - **First floor bathroom addition** (if structurally feasible)\n   - **Stairlift installation** (temporary solution, but does not work with wheelchair)\n   - **Relocation to an accessible single-story unit** — may be the most practical long-term solution\n2. **Roll-in shower**: With shower wheelchair or tub bench with handheld showerhead\n3. **Grab bars**: Throughout bathroom, at bed, at exterior doors\n4. **Door widening**: Minimum 32 inches for wheelchair clearance (36 inches preferred)\n5. **Ramp**: For exterior entry\n6. **Kitchen modifications**: Lowered countertop section, accessible storage, side-opening oven\n7. **Hospital bed or adjustable bed**: For positioning, transfers, and skin protection\n\n**Upper Body Preservation Program — Critical Priority**:\nHer upper extremities are her lifeline for independence. Shoulder injury from manual wheelchair overuse is preventable.\n- **Rotator cuff strengthening**: External rotation, scapular stabilization exercises, resistance bands\n- **Ergonomic propulsion training**: If using manual wheelchair, long smooth strokes (semicircular pattern) rather than short choppy strokes to reduce repetitive stress\n- **Stretching**: Pectoralis, anterior shoulder, neck — prevent forward-rolled shoulder posture from wheelchair seating\n- **Minimize manual wheelchair propulsion**: The power wheelchair prescription will be the most important intervention for shoulder preservation\n- **Weight management**: Every pound impacts wheelchair propulsion difficulty and transfer effort\n\n**Fatigue Management — Multimodal Approach**:\n1. **Energy conservation training**: OT-led program teaching pacing, prioritization, and planning. Break activities into segments with rest periods. Schedule demanding tasks during peak energy hours (usually morning for MS).\n2. **Cooling strategies**: MS fatigue worsens dramatically with heat (Uhthoff phenomenon). Cooling vest, air-conditioned environment, cool beverages.\n3. **Sleep optimization**: Address nocturia (timed fluid restriction, bedside catheterization), treat restless legs if present, assess for sleep apnea.\n4. **Pharmacologic**: Amantadine 100 mg BID (first-line for MS fatigue). Modafinil 200 mg each morning (second-line). Avoid stimulants that disrupt sleep.\n5. **Exercise**: Paradoxically, structured low-to-moderate intensity exercise (aquatic therapy is ideal) improves MS fatigue over time.\n6. **Depression treatment**: Fatigue and depression overlap significantly — treating depression often improves fatigue.\n\n**Spasticity Management**:\n- **Current regimen review**: Optimize oral medications — baclofen (titrate to 20 mg TID if tolerated, monitor sedation), add tizanidine 4 mg at bedtime for nocturnal spasms\n- **Stretching program**: Daily passive ROM, especially hip adductors, hamstrings, ankle plantarflexors. Caregiver or self-directed with straps.\n- **Standing program**: If a standing frame is tolerable, 30-60 minutes daily reduces spasticity and maintains bone density\n- **Botulinum toxin**: For focal spasticity interfering with function (ankle clonus impeding footrest positioning, adductor spasticity impeding catheterization)\n- **Intrathecal baclofen pump**: Consider if oral medications are insufficient or side effects are limiting. ITB is highly effective for lower extremity spasticity in MS and can dramatically improve comfort, positioning, and catheterization access.',
    ),
