    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the wheelchair funding process, insurance appeals strategy, and home accessibility resources.',
      content: '**Power Wheelchair Insurance Funding — Medicare and Private Insurance**:\n\n**Medicare Coverage Criteria for Power Mobility Devices (PMDs)**:\nMedicare requires a comprehensive, multi-step process:\n1. **Face-to-face examination**: Must be performed by the treating physician (or NP/PA) within 45 days before the wheelchair prescription. Must document that the patient\'s condition is expected to last at least 3 years and that a power wheelchair is needed for use in the home.\n2. **Seven-element order**: Written prescription with specific elements including diagnosis, wheelchair type, accessories, and reason the patient cannot use a manual wheelchair (or why a manual wheelchair would be insufficient).\n3. **Specialty evaluation**: Performed by PT/OT who is a certified ATP (Assistive Technology Professional). This seating and mobility evaluation generates a detailed report documenting functional limitations and wheelchair specifications.\n4. **Medical necessity documentation**: Must demonstrate that the patient (a) has a mobility limitation that significantly impairs participation in mobility-related ADLs in the home, (b) the limitation cannot be resolved by a cane, walker, or manual wheelchair, and (c) the patient has the cognitive and physical ability to safely operate a power wheelchair.\n\n**Common Reasons for Denial and How to Address Them**:\n- **"Insufficient documentation"**: This is the most common denial. The physician letter must specifically state how the patient\'s condition prevents safe use of a manual wheelchair (upper extremity fatigue, fall risk, shoulder preservation) and how a power wheelchair will improve function IN THE HOME (Medicare focuses on home use, not community mobility).\n- **"Patient can use a manual wheelchair"**: Document that she can only propel 100 feet before fatigue, cannot manage inclines or rough surfaces, and continued manual propulsion risks shoulder injury that would render her unable to transfer or self-catheterize.\n- **"Scooter would be sufficient"**: Document that a scooter lacks tilt/recline for pressure management, does not provide adequate postural support for her trunk weakness and lateral lean, and does not accommodate her spasticity management needs.\n\n**Appeals Process**:\n1. **First-level appeal (Redetermination)**: Submit within 120 days. Include additional documentation — a detailed letter from the physiatrist, the ATP evaluation, photographs of current ill-fitting wheelchair, documentation of falls and injuries.\n2. **Second-level appeal (Reconsideration by QIC)**: If redetermination is denied. Include peer-reviewed evidence on power wheelchair outcomes in progressive MS.\n3. **Third-level appeal (ALJ Hearing)**: Administrative Law Judge hearing. The patient and physician can testify. Success rates at ALJ hearings are significantly higher than lower-level appeals.\n4. **Congressional inquiry**: Contact the patient\'s congressional representative — constituent services offices can advocate with CMS.\n\n**Home Accessibility Funding Resources**:\n- **State Assistive Technology Programs**: Every state has a federally funded AT program that provides equipment loans, financial assistance, and information\n- **National MS Society**: Home modification grants, equipment assistance, financial assistance for medical costs\n- **VA Specially Adapted Housing (SAH) grants**: If the patient has any military service history\n- **USDA Rural Development grants**: Section 504 Home Repair Program for rural homeowners\n- **State vocational rehabilitation**: May fund home modifications as part of independent living plan\n- **Habitat for Humanity**: Some chapters offer home modification programs for people with disabilities\n- **Community Development Block Grants**: Through local government — some municipalities fund accessibility modifications\n- **Tax deductions**: Medical expense deductions for home modifications that are medically necessary (IRS Publication 502)\n\n**Transition Planning**:\n- The patient may need to relocate to an accessible unit. Social work referral to assist with accessible housing search, Section 8 housing vouchers if income-eligible, and local accessible housing registries.',
    ),
