    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you would address her grief over loss of walking while maintaining engagement in rehabilitation.',
      content: '**Responding to Grief Over Mobility Loss**:\n\nThis is a pivotal moment. The patient is expressing genuine grief — the loss of walking is experienced as a death of her former identity. Dismissing this grief or rushing to "fix it" with positive reframing will damage the therapeutic alliance. But allowing her to remain in despair without offering a path forward would be equally harmful.\n\n**Step 1 — Sit with the grief. Do not rush past it.**\n\nI would pull my chair close, make eye contact, and allow silence before speaking.\n\n\'I hear you. And I want you to know that what you are feeling right now is not weakness — it is a completely natural response to a very real loss. Losing the ability to walk is a loss, and it deserves to be grieved. You do not have to pretend this is okay.\'\n\n**Step 2 — Acknowledge her identity loss specifically.**\n\n\'You told me when we started that you used to take care of other people — that was your career, your identity. And now you feel like you cannot take care of yourself. I imagine that shift is one of the most painful parts of all of this.\'\n\n**Allow her to respond. Listen without interrupting.**\n\n**Step 3 — Gently challenge the narrative without invalidating the grief.**\n\n\'I want to push back gently on one thing you said — that you have lost. Because from where I am sitting, I see a woman who has fought this disease for 18 years. Who adapted when she had to give up nursing. Who lives independently. Who manages her own catheterization, her own medications, her own daily life. That is not someone who has lost.\n\nThe wheelchair is not the end of a fight. It is a different tool for continuing one. I have seen patients in your situation go from being trapped in their homes — afraid to go anywhere because of falls — to getting out in the community, traveling, volunteering, re-engaging with life, because a properly fitted power wheelchair gave them the freedom to do things they had stopped doing.\'\n\n**Step 4 — Reframe the wheelchair as independence, not dependence.**\n\n\'Right now, you are borrowing a wheelchair that does not fit you, that exhausts your arms, and that cannot get you where you need to go. That is not a wheelchair — that is a cage. What we are going to get you is a tool that gives you back your independence — that protects your arms so you can keep doing your own care, that takes you where you want to go without falling, and that fits your body so you are comfortable and safe.\'\n\n**Step 5 — Address the "why bother" with concrete, meaningful goals.**\n\n\'You asked why you should bother. Let me ask you something — if you could go to your daughter\'s apartment for dinner without worrying about falling, would that matter to you? If you could go to the grocery store, or to a park, or to a movie, on your own schedule — would that matter?\n\nBecause those are the things we are working toward. Not walking — I cannot promise that. But living. Fully, safely, on your terms.\'\n\n**Step 6 — Offer professional support for grief processing.**\n\n\'I also want to connect you with a psychologist who works with people navigating exactly this kind of transition. Grief does not have to be carried alone, and having someone to talk to who understands what this kind of loss feels like can make a real difference.\n\nThe National MS Society also has peer support programs — connecting you with other women with MS who have been through this same transition and come out the other side. Would you be open to that?\'\n\n**Step 7 — Establish partnership.**\n\n\'Here is what I need from you: I need you to show up. To the wheelchair evaluation, to PT, to the home assessment. You do not have to feel hopeful every day. You do not have to pretend this does not hurt. But I need you to keep showing up, because the work we do together only works if you are in it with me. Can we agree on that?\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating grief without wallowing, acknowledging identity loss, gently challenging the catastrophic narrative with evidence of her strength, reframing the wheelchair as freedom rather than defeat, providing concrete motivating goals, offering professional mental health support, and establishing a collaborative partnership.',
